From 330affe3a6ef204435876934cd2f3c9b33b97315 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:39:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Detect compound-array and builtin quoted-subscript
 arithmetic sinks

Squash of the five reviewed commits of PR #2788 plus the linear-time fix for review
5905734191, rebased onto main 854519ff5a (after #2981, which also edits
tools/shell_arith_sink.py). Original commit messages follow in order.

----

[daveey] Detect compound array and quoted builtin subscript sinks

Proof: baseline d9e85d65 has 22 failing must-sink cases; targeted suites pass 159 tests with the fix. Preserve numeric date exemption policy and ordinary quoted data controls.

[daveey] Keep arithmetic expansion dispatch within complexity budget

[daveey] Resolve builtin command position before quoted subscript sinks

Address PR #2788 R1: share reserved-word peeling with lifecycle command scans; recognize assignments, command/builtin options, redirections and continuations before quoted variable names. Keep echo data and command introspection non-sinks.

Proof: 52 new failures on 56dfe1b01c, 148 targeted tests pass after fix including 10 actual Bash recursive marker executions. Nine exact-anchor classifier mutants killed. Broad pre-redirection selection: 2784 passed; 2400-case corpus: zero violations and false refusals. Canonical ship rebases stale base before final static gate and exact-head review.

fix(guard): scope quoted subscript sinks to command frames [daveey]

Review R1/R2: raw character adjacency rejected quote-concatenated names, while full-prefix tokenization inherited outer substitution quotes. Use command-frame source offsets and independent backtick scopes; let builtin target recognition handle quote boundaries.

Proof: 26 RED regressions at 86d7baf, GREEN 1713 guard tests. Generated 140 real-Bash sink executions and 70 inert controls; baseline misses all 140, fixed code misses none. Adjacency/full-prefix/backtick mutants killed. Shape corpus and all static checks green.

fix(guard): preserve expansion words and fail closed on unknown consumers [daveey]

R1: prefix re-tokenization treated completed expansion punctuation as outer command boundaries. Mask nested expansion frames before tokenization and default unresolved consumers to arithmetic; exempt only proven data/introspection/redirection positions. Unsupported case grammar fails closed.

Proof: prior-head RED 25 failures; generated prior/builtin/name/wrapper Bash oracle and guard regression green. Includes review and shepherd expansion variants, inert controls, and date watcher preservation.

[daveey] Keep quoted-subscript classification linear and command-scoped

Review 5905734191 (t_8794b5573bc87df3) found substitution_in_arithmetic
re-tokenized the whole frame prefix for every double-quoted '[', making the
lifecycle scan quadratic (1500 echo "[..]" lines: 26.8 s vs 0.72 s base).

- Command frames now track the start of their current simple command: unquoted
  ';', '&', '|' and newline (and the end of a heredoc body) reset it, so
  quoted_name_is_arithmetic only sees its own command. Redirection '&'/'|'
  (>&, <&, &>, >|) do not reset.
- A quoted '[' is classified only when the character before it (past quote
  concatenation) can end a name or an expansion; whitespace, '=', ':', '.', '/'
  and similar never form a subscript (real bash 5: inert), which also drops the
  curl -d "[$(..)]" / git commit -m "fix [$(..)]" false positives (nit 3).
- The classification is cached per quoted word, so a long python -c "..." word
  is read once.
- The compound-assignment check walks back over the name instead of searching
  command[:start] for every '('.

Linear-time accounting tests (deterministic call/length counts) are RED at
2d42734f9f (file times out at 300 s; python word and 4 inert-name controls
fail) and GREEN here. Sinks across ;/&&/||/|/&/newline/heredoc and quote/
expansion concatenation stay sinks under a real-bash marker oracle.

Bench (scan_gateway_lifecycle), this head vs 2d42734f9f: deploy 1500 lines
1.38 s vs 43.8 s; echo "[INFO]" x1000 0.70 s vs 12.6 s; 8 KB python -c
0.12 s vs 1.21 s. Guard suites: 39 files, 8639 passed, 0 failed.
